3.Your Redis cache has a hit rate of 92%. Under a flash sale it drops to 31% in 40 seconds and your DB starts receiving 60k QPS it 
was never designed for. Walk through exactly what happened and how you'd prevent it

What happened?

Before flash sale:

Clients → Service → Redis → DB
             |
          92% hits
          8% DB traffic

Redis was serving most requests, DB was healthy.



During flash sale:

Millions of users started requesting the same hot products.

Example:

GET product:iphone15
GET product:iphone15
GET product:iphone15
...
Cache entries were missing or expired.



Possible reasons:

New keys appeared that Redis never had.
Flash sale products were not preloaded into Redis.
TTL expired at the same time (cache avalanche).
New product inventory/price data changed and cache was invalidated.



Prevention 1: Cache Warmup (for predictable flash sales)

For flash sales, we know the hot products beforehand.

Before sale starts:

Product Catalog
       |
       |
 Cache Warmup Job
       |
       v
 Redis


                            
Example:

SET product:123 {
 id:123,
 price:999,
 inventory:500
}
TTL: 2 hours

So when sale starts:

Request
 |
 Redis HIT
 |
 Response

No DB load spike.




                            
Prevention 2: Cache Stampede Protection (for unexpected misses)

Suppose:

product:123 expired

1000 requests come simultaneously.

Without protection:

Request 1 ---> DB
Request 2 ---> DB
Request 3 ---> DB
...
Request 1000 --> DB

DB gets 1000 identical queries.


                                         
With stampede protection:

Only one request rebuilds cache.

Example:

Request 1
   |
 Redis MISS
   |
Acquire lock
   |
Fetch DB
   |
Update Redis
   |
Release lock


Request 2-1000
   |
 Redis MISS
   |
 Wait for lock
   |
Retry Redis
   |
Get cached value



                                         
Implementation:
Use Redis distributed lock:

SET lock:product:123 true NX EX 10
NX → only one instance gets lock
EX → auto expiry





Prevention 3: Rate limit / protect DB

Even with caching failures, DB should not die.

Add:

API rate limiting
Queue requests
Circuit breaker
Bulkhead isolation


                                         
Example:

100k requests/sec

        Service

        |
        |
   Rate limiter

        |
        |
      DB

Allow only 10k QPS                                         



 
Prevention 4: Inventory data needs special handling                                         

 For flash sale inventory:
Don't cache the source of truth.

Bad:

Redis inventory = 10
DB inventory = 10

User buys
Redis decremented
DB later updated

Can oversell.



Better:

Redis:
fast availability check

DB:
final reservation with atomic update



Example:

UPDATE inventory
SET available = available - 1
WHERE product_id=123



"The cache hit rate dropped because flash sale traffic introduced a huge number of cache misses. 
Either the hot items were not warmed up, or existing keys expired causing a cache avalanche. 
Every miss went to the database, creating a thundering herd and pushing DB QPS beyond capacity. 
I would prevent this using cache warmup for predictable hot products, cache stampede protection using Redis locks, 
stale-while-revalidate to serve old data while refreshing asynchronously, 
and database protection using rate limiting and circuit breakers."
AND available > 0;


